3:## Normative Modeling of Thalamic Nuclear Volumes and Characterization of Lateralized Volume Alterations in Alzheimer's Disease Versus Schizophrenia
9:## Normative Modeling
11:Very few comparisons of predictive models for normative modeling have been reported (Ge at al.). We compared 3 different predictive models - ordinary least squares regression (OLS), multiple fractional polynomial regression (MFP), and generalized additive models of location shape and scale (GAMLSS) (Bethlehem et al. 2022; Dima et al. 2022; Dinga et al. 2021; Bozek et al. 2023) which have been previously described for normative modeling. For each method, we tested a univariate and a multivariate model. In the univariate models, age was the predictive variable, and the models were trained on sex stratified data sets following eTIV volume adjustment, site harmonization with Combat, and mean-centering. A 5-fold crossvalidation was used to evaluate each model. All models were implemented in R and trained for each region independently. The model with the best overall performance based on average mean absolute error (MAE) was selected and trained on all control samples for each region.
13:Ordinary least squares regression (OLS) was implemented using the lm function in base R and takes the following forms for univariate and multivariate models:
21:Where 𝑋 is a continuous variable, 𝑝 is a power from set S, and 𝑖 is the specified degrees of freedom. mfp uses an iterative backward selection method to keep only significant variables and powers. Here, we used the maximum degrees of freedom i =4. Univariate and multivariate models were as follows:
25:Generalized additive models of location shape and scale (GAMLSS) was implemented using the gamlss, cs, and random functions from the gamlss package in R. Briefly, gamlss models the mean ( 𝜇 ), standard deviation ( 𝜎 ), skew ( 𝜈 ), and curtosis ( 𝜏 ) over a distribution, cs fits a cubic smoothing spline, and random fits random effects. The GAMLSS models take the following forms:
29:Where 𝑆𝐻𝐴𝑆𝐻 is the sinh-arcsinh distribution, 𝜇௨ is a univariate model, 𝜇௠ is a multivariate model, 𝑓 ௖௦ is a cubic smoothing spline, and 𝑍 are random effects intercepts.
33:In the primary analyses, z-score distributions of cases were compared to controls using the z-test. Using the same methodology, we carried out supplemental pairwise comparisons between all cases / phenotypes (e.g. AD to EMCI, EMCI to LMCI, and AD to LMCI). We also performed supplemental analyses on laterality. The laterality index (LI) was calculated for all subjects for each region using the formula LI = (VL - VR)/(VL+VR) where VL is the volume of the region on the left side and VR is the volume of the region on the right side. As previously described, the top-performing model was trained on the control subjects, z-scores were calculated for both cases and controls, z-score distributions were compared using the z-test, and p-values were adjusted using the Benjamini-Hochberg method (Benjamini and Hochberg 1995). Additionally, we compared z-score distributions of left sided regions to right sided regions within each phenotype.
75:In univariate models (see main text and below), volumes were harmonized across sites in sex stratified datasets using Combat as implemented in Neuroharmonize python library (16-18). To reduce the possibility of removing variation related to disease state rather than site, site effects were first estimated using control subjects and harmonization was then applied to both cases and controls. Alternatively, site was included as a covariate in multivariate models and modeled as a fixed effect in OLS and MFP models and as a random effect in GAMLSS. With the exception of ADNI, site refers to the physical scanner used to obtain the imaging. Despite using the subset of subjects described in Bernstein et al. with MRI acquisition on a Siemens 3T scanner (15), the number of ADNI sites was 44 making harmonization algorithms suboptimal due to small n per site and some sites without control samples (Supplemental Table 5). Since the acquisition protocol was harmonized across Siemens 3T scanners, we could treat ADNI as a single site. Recent analyses by Vidal et al. using the Frequently Traveling Human Phantom MRI dataset
81:To assess the effect of individual variables on model performance, we compared the performance of models using age only, age plus one additional variable (e.g. sex, TIV, or site), and all variables using the univariate and multivariate GAMLSS models specified above. For example, the effect of TIV on model performance was assessed using a univariate approach where age was the independent variable, and volumes were adjusted for TIV or the multivariate approach where age was the independent variable and TIV was included as a covariate. As above, 5-fold cross validation was used to compare models and MAE averaged over all folds and nuclei was used as the primary metric of model performance.
85:The microbenchmark R package was used to evaluate training times for all models. Each model was trained 20 times on the control samples using volumes for the left whole thalamus. Training times were then averaged. The hardware was an Apple MacBook Pro with Apple M1 Max processor (8 performance cores, 2 efficiency cores) with 64 GB of ram.
89:## Normative Modeling
91:Six univariate and multivariate models using ordinary least squares regression (OLS), multiple fractional polynomial regression (MFP), and generalized additive models of location, shape, and scale (GAMLSS) were compared with 5-fold cross-validation across the 22 thalamic regions for a total of 660 trained models. Global mean absolute error (MAE) was comparable amongst all models (Supplemental Table 1). GAMLSS models were the overall top performing models based on MAE. MAE values between test and train datasets were not significantly different as assessed by a two-tailed t-test applied across folds and regions (minimum p=0.93), ruling out overfitting. Z-scores for all three models were highly concordant (Supplmental Figures 8-10). In the case of the left medial geniculate nucleus, it appears z-scores from OLS and MFP are nearly identical suggesting that the optimal model chosen by MFP was linear (Supplemental Figure 10). Although model performance was similar and GAMLSS requires increased training times, there are benefits to using a GAMLSS multivariate model including increasing the public accessibility of our normative models by elimination of steps such as sex-stratification, eTIV adjustment, and combat harmonization and the ability to leverage multiple methods described to adapt a GAMLSS model to new data (1,3).
93:Model calibration plots for the GAMLSS multivariate model were visually assessed and did not reveal substantial miscalibration (left Pulvinar shown in Supplemental Figure 11 as an example). Visual inspection of z-scores and volumes demonstrated that the model mitigated effects of age, site, sex, and estimated total intracranial volume (eTIV) as evidenced by near zero slopes for controls and shifting means for cases (Supplemental Figures 6 and 7).
107:Training times were 3-4 times longer for multivariate models compared to univariate models and a GAMLSS multivariate models required about 14 seconds in training time whereas an MFP model required about 0.2 seconds to train (Supplemental Table 9).
111:Adjusting for eTIV had the largest individual effect on model performance followed by sex while site had the smallest individual effect on model performance (Supplemental Figure 2). Model performance was similar regardless of the method of adjusting for TIV or site (Supplemental figure 2, Supplemental Table 1).
127:Supplemental Figure 2 - Comparison of the effects of covariates on GAMLSS model performance using 5-fold cross-validation. Mean average error (MAE) and Root mean squared error (RMSE) were averaged over all folds, thalamic nuclei, and sexes. eTIV - estimated total intracranial volume used as a covariate. eTIV-r - volumes were adjusted for eTIV using the residual method. Combat - site harmonization was performed using Combat rather than site as a
131:Supplemental Figure 3 - Centile plots of age versus eTIV adjusted volumes for all thalamic regions in sex stratified datasets (i.e. a univariate model). Combat has been applied to mitigate site effects. Thalamus - whole thalamus; AV - anteroventral nucleus; VA - ventral anterio nucleus; VLa - ventral lateral anterior nucleus; VLp - ventral lateral posterior nucleus; VPL ventral posterior lateral nucleus; Pul - pulvinar nucleus; LGN - lateral geniculate nucleus; MGN - medial geniculate nucleus; CM - centromedian nucleus; MD/Pf - medial dorsal-parafascicular nucleus.
205:Supplemental Figure 6 - Raw volumes and z-scores for representative regions generated using the multivariate GAMLSS model. Thalamus - whole thalamus; VPL - ventral posterior lateral nucleus; CM - centromedian nucleus.
213:Supplemental Figure 8 - Scatterplots of z-scores for both cases and controls generated from GAMLSS multivariate model and MFP multivariate model. Thalamus - whole thalamus; AV anteroventral nucleus; VA - ventral anterio nucleus; VLa - ventral lateral anterior nucleus; VLp - ventral lateral posterior nucleus; VPL - ventral posterior lateral nucleus; Pul - pulvinar nucleus; LGN - lateral geniculate nucleus; MGN - medial geniculate nucleus; CM centromedian nucleus; MD/Pf - medial dorsal-parafascicular nucleus.

